<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-5.7pt;"><img src="2610.20538v1/Figures/icons/prismacoder-0924-1-trim.png" id="g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:55/48;" width="55" height="48" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></span></span>
</span>
<span id="id2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:281.0pt;">
<span id="id2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal</span>
<span id="id2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis</span>
</span></h1>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal code intelligence connects the symbolic world of programs with the
visual world their execution creates. Interest in this capability is
burgeoning, as models are increasingly asked to produce charts, webpages,
animations, and semi-structured documents. Recently, proprietary models have
pushed far ahead, producing interactive artifacts even from a text description
or a screenshot alone. Yet their data and training remain closed, and
open progress is held back by the quantity and quality of available
data and by the pipelines and methods for producing it. Quality is the harder
of the two, since a sample must be correct as a program, well built, and above
all visually right once rendered. No existing signal covers all three.
Execution often returns just a pass or fail, code similarity does not map to the
visual output, and task-specific verifiers do not generalize. To push open
progress further, we start from the data side with <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span>, a hierarchical
data pipeline centered on a synthesized rubric that decomposes these factors
into checkable criteria. The same rubric serves as both
filter and reward: it curates the supervised corpus, then drives
reinforcement learning on visual-coding tasks. Leveraging <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> for data
synthesis and filtering, we build <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M, the most diverse open dataset for
multimodal code intelligence and the largest to date. On the model side, we train <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B and
<span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B on this data and release both. They lead the open state of
the art by a wide margin and rival leading proprietary systems.
Our analyses further show that the proposed rubric-based pipeline improves multimodal coding performance across model backbones.
We release our code, model, and data at the
<a href="https://qiushisun.github.io/PrismaCoder-Home/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">PrismaCoder Homepage</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">1. Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.20538v1/task_overview_figure.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/168;" width="452" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Scenarios and formats covered by <span id="S1.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M and <span id="S1.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From symbols to pixels, code intelligence now extends beyond textual source code
into the visual world that programs produce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. A growing range of applications
specifies intent through images and judges a result by what its code renders, from charts and web pages to
diagrams, typeset documents, and animations. Leading
proprietary systems now produce interactive artifacts from a text description or
a screenshot alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prior open efforts have mostly addressed one corner of this space at a time, from
plotting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galimzyanov et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
and chart-to-code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025c</a>)</cite> to
screenshot-to-page generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Each
comes with its own data and recipe,
making it difficult for the resulting models to generalize across task domains and output formats.
Recently, open work such as
JanusCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite> has brought many of these scenarios under a
shared visual-programmatic interface, with follow-up refinements building on the
same view <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Despite these advances, publicly available training data remains limited in scale and uneven in quality, with only a handful of open releases and resources to date <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
The pipelines used to build these datasets are rarely released, while proprietary systems keep their data and training closed.
Moreover, existing public data are typically curated with either a single per-sample score, which provides limited diagnostic information about errors, or a task-specific verifier, which does not generalize across tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span>, a rubric-based framework for multimodal code data curation and policy optimization. Rather than relying solely on a holistic judgment or a fixed checklist, <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> applies a common evaluation procedure across data sources and task domains while tailoring its criteria to each instruction. Each rubric combines reusable domain-level checks with instruction-specific requirements, with the number of criteria adjusted to the estimated task difficulty. Code-level and visual-level criteria are evaluated separately on the generated program and its rendered artifact, respectively.
These scores guide data curation and serve as reinforcement learning rewards.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span>, we curate <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M, the broadest open dataset to date for advancing multimodal code intelligence, spanning more than 12 categories and
10 programming languages with both text-centric and vision-centric data. To build this coverage, we draw seeds from complementary sources: public visual-coding corpora cover common plotting and graphics tasks; research figures and open-source repositories contribute long-tail scientific examples; and web, interactive-demo, and chemical-structure sources broaden the range of artifacts. We then convert the seeds into self-contained tasks, check candidate programs through format-specific execution and rendering, and use instruction-specific rubric scores to filter or refine the resulting examples. This gives the dataset both breadth of coverage and a consistent quality-control process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To assess whether the breadth of <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M translates into broad model capability, we develop <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B and <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B.
Evaluated on a wide range of representative benchmarks, <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span> leads the existing open state of the art by a wide margin and matches the proprietary systems to a considerable degree (<a href="#S5" title="5. Experiments ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
Through extensive analyses (<a href="#S6" title="6. Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we show that the data and pipeline generalize across base models beyond our release. We further show that rubric-guided curation outperforms direct-judge selection and that rubric scores align closely with human judgments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposed Rubric Synthesis for Visual-coding Data.</span> We introduce
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span>, a principled pipeline whose hierarchically synthesized rubrics
serve as both filter and reward. Based on it, we curate <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M, the broadest and largest open
dataset to date.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open Models that Rival Proprietary Systems.</span> We build <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B/35B on
<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M, which lead the open state of the art by a wide margin and match or surpass
leading proprietary systems on charts, front-end pages, and semi-structured documents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Insights across Data, Modeling, and Training.</span>
Beyond data and model contributions, we further share insights through extensive analysis. It shows that our pipeline generalizes across scenarios and backbones and yields artifacts better aligned with human preference.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">2. Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal Code Intelligence.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Code intelligence now extends beyond text-only programming to visual-programmatic tasks, where intent may be visual <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and outputs can be judged only after execution or rendering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
On the output side, syntactic validity is insufficient; correctness also depends on layout, semantics, interaction, and editability.
On the input side, reading values and layout off renders remains a perception bottleneck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masry et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite> across vision-language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
The tasks run from screenshot-to-code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to front-end engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> and generative UI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>; chart generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>; SVG and symbolic graphics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nishina &amp; Matsui, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> with their structural probes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>; and interactive artifacts and code-driven demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ku et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
Recent generalists unify these domains via JanusCoder’s visual-programmatic interface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, VisCodex’s vision–code integration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, VisCoder2’s agentic visualization coding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and VinciCoder’s visual reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
Yet a shared modeling interface does not by itself make heterogeneous notions of correctness consistently measurable or usable for training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.
Complementing this progress, we contribute on the data side, with <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M spanning the broadest scenarios and open data types.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning Signals for Code Generation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conventional code tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> draw training data from instruction synthesis and evolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
For many such tasks, executable tests provide a cheap, reproducible signal for data filtering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> and reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Visual outputs break this assumption: successful execution confirms only that the code runs, not that its render is faithful, well composed, or semantically correct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Visual benchmarks instead use task-specific render checks for charts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, visual and structural similarity for web pages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or judge protocols for interactive artifacts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; effective individually, these measures do not compose across artifacts.
Recent work moves toward finer supervision, from JanusCoder’s single-pass rubric over fixed dimensions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite> to VinciCoder’s coarse-to-fine rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Neither signal is yet both fine enough to localize failures and general enough to cover every task, at curation and at training alike. In this work, one synthesized rubric per instruction serves both roles, filtering the training data and rewarding reinforcement learning across every format we cover.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">3. PrismaForge</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span>, a rubric-based scoring framework that lies in the center of our data curation and training pipeline.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.20538v1/pipeline_figure.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/198;" width="452" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">PrismaForge<span id="S3.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> writes one rubric per instruction and uses it twice, to filter
candidate data into </span>PrismaCoder<span id="S3.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">-2M and to reward rollouts in reinforcement learning.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a large-scale coding dataset prepared for visualization or demonstration, the key challenge is to determine which candidates are sufficiently correct and useful to retain. Unlike problems with easily verifiable outputs, visualization quality is often specified only implicitly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. A single holistic score is therefore hard to verify and offers little insight into why a candidate succeeds or fails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A natural alternative is to evaluate candidates using explicit criteria, but a fixed rubric is too coarse for diverse visualization tasks. Different instructions may prioritize numerical accuracy and axis scales, visual similarity, layout, or artifact editability. A universal rubric must stay broad and may miss the properties most important for a specific example. At the other extreme, manually designing separate rubrics for every visualization instruction would require substantial human effort and is hard to scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To balance these two extremes, we introduce a sample-specific rubric system that combines reusable domain-level criteria with criteria synthesized specifically for each instruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> index dataset instances, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the visualization domain associated with instance <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. We represent candidate sample <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as a tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},c_{i},v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},c_{i},v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the instruction, which may contain text and optional visual references; <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the response code produced for that instruction; and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}=\operatorname{Exec}(c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Exec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}=\operatorname{Exec}(c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the visual artifact obtained by executing <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate each candidate using two complementary sets of rubric criteria: code rubrics <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which assess properties that can be verified from <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and visual rubrics <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which assess properties directly observable from <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:124.2pt;">
<p id="S3.F3.3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.F3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="--ltx-border-color:#B3B3B3;"><img src="2610.20538v1/Figures/appendix/rubric_case_1_gold.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:241/136;" width="241" height="136" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<p id="S3.F3.3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.F3.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Reference</span></p>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:107.0pt;">
