<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Scored separately, the vision rubric catches a misplaced footer that every code
check and a holistic judge let through.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows why the two views are kept apart. The synthesized page
carries the reference’s content, colors, and layout in its code, so every code check passes.
Its footer, however, is drawn over the Services section and clips the third button. A holistic
judge still rates the page 78 out of 100, above about four fifths of the pool, whereas the
vision rubric scores it 0.18. The checks and judgments behind both scores are listed in
Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Rubric Synthesis Prompts and Examples ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Within each modality, we further distinguish between domain-shared and sample-specific criteria. Domain-shared rubrics encode requirements that recur across samples within the same visualization domain, whereas sample-specific rubrics are derived from the instruction <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and capture requirements unique to the current sample. Thus, for modality <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{c,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{c,v\}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{shared}}^{(m)}(d_{i})\cup\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{spec}}^{(m)}(x_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>shared</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>spec</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{shared}}^{(m)}(d_{i})\cup\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{spec}}^{(m)}(x_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Because individual criteria may differ in importance, each rubric item <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned a nonnegative, sample-dependent weight <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,r}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,r}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">To score a candidate,
let <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(m)}(r,y)\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(m)}(r,y)\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the judge score indicating how well artifact <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies rubric criterion <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> under modality <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. The normalized score for modality <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is then</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(m)}=\frac{1}{\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}}w_{i,r}^{(m)}}\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}}w_{i,r}^{(m)}f^{(m)}(r,y_{i}^{(m)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(m)}=\frac{1}{\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}}w_{i,r}^{(m)}}\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(m)}}w_{i,r}^{(m)}f^{(m)}(r,y_{i}^{(m)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The two modality scores are then combined into an overall sample score with appropriate weighting</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}=\lambda_{c}S_{i}^{(c)}+\lambda_{v}S_{i}^{(v)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}=\lambda_{c}S_{i}^{(c)}+\lambda_{v}S_{i}^{(v)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c},\lambda_{v}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c},\lambda_{v}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}+\lambda_{v}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}+\lambda_{v}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, candidate samples vary substantially in difficulty and complexity, and therefore should not be evaluated with the same number of rubric items. We first assign each instruction <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> a difficulty level <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and use this level to guide rubric generation. Each difficulty band is associated with a bounded range for the number of generated criteria. This allows more complex samples to receive finer-grained evaluation while keeping the rubric compact enough to control judging cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">3.2. Rubric-Based Policy Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The same rubric framework can also be used to construct rewards for reinforcement learning. Because rubric scores are normalized within each modality and aggregated using a common scoring rule, they provide a consistent reward scale across samples with different numbers and types of criteria. We therefore use the rubric-based evaluator as a structured reward function during policy optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each prompt <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> rollout responses from the behavior policy</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{c_{i}^{(1)},\cdots,c_{i}^{(K)}}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(\cdot\mid x_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>old</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{c_{i}^{(1)},\cdots,c_{i}^{(K)}}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(\cdot\mid x_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Each rollout <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is executed to obtain the visual artifacts <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{(k)}=\textrm{Exec}\left(c_{i}^{(k)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Exec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{(k)}=\textrm{Exec}\left(c_{i}^{(k)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Rollouts that failed to execute or render receive reward <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The judge model then scores these rollouts. For each successful rollout trajectory <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},c_{i}^{(k)},v_{i}^{(k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},c_{i}^{(k)},v_{i}^{(k)})</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply its accompany rubrics set <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(c)},\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(v)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(c)},\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(v)})</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain the aggregated score <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> via Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–Eq.<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. These scores are then leveraged to compute the within-group normalized advantage</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i}^{(k)}=\frac{s_{i}^{(k)}-\mu_{i}}{\sigma_{i}+\epsilon}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i}^{(k)}=\frac{s_{i}^{(k)}-\mu_{i}}{\sigma_{i}+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}s_{i}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}s_{i}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the standard deviation of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{s_{i}^{(k)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{s_{i}^{(k)}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">With this reward formulation, the policy can then be optimized with the standard GRPO
clipped objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In practice, we also used GSPO objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> with Rollout Routing Replay techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for the MoE models to stabilize experts training.</p>
</div>
