<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M with <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> by collecting seeds from complementary sources, gating
them by rubric score into a supervised corpus, and holding out a subset for reinforcement
learning, where the same rubrics supply the reward.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">4.1. Data Sourcing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal code generation needs several capabilities at once, so we collect four kinds of
seeds that each cover one of them and train on them together (<a href="#S4.T1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ 4. PrismaCoder-2M ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tabs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ 4. PrismaCoder-2M ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visualization Fundamentals.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Visual coding starts from a core of plotting skills,
such as mapping data to axes and marks and composing legends and layered plots. Public plotting
datasets build this foundation with instruction–code and visualization–code pairs in
Python <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hunter, 2007</a>)</cite> and in SVG, Mermaid, Vega-Lite, and other visualization
languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diverse Scientific Artifacts.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scientific work produces more varied visuals than
standard charts, from multi-panel figures to domain-specific encodings. Plotting code from
open-source GitHub repositories and figures from arXiv papers bring this variety into the data,
and an AST-based pass splits long or monolithic scripts into self-contained units.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Front-End Layout and Interaction.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Front-end development asks a model to arrange
many elements on a page and to make them respond to users. Web pages from
WebCode2M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and the MCD dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> supply the
layout, while interactive demos supply the behavior. The demos are adapted from prior
releases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite> or rewritten from static pages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laurençon et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
and complex generation prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> into tasks of varying
visual, functional, and reasoning complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Symbolic Notation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Science, mathematics, and music communicate through symbolic
notation, where a single wrong symbol breaks the result. Chemical-structure code seeded from
PubChem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite> must describe a valid molecule, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span>, Asymptote, LilyPond, and Manim programs must compile into well-formed
documents, figures, scores, and scenes. The first three formats draw on the same sources as the
plots, and the Manim seeds come from the 3Blue1Brown video code.
See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Data Sources ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">4.2. Iterative Data Gating</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every seed enters <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> as a candidate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},c_{i},v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},c_{i},v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Where a seed lacks working code, a curation model <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> generates it, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{i}\sim\mathcal{L}^{C}(\cdot\mid x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\sim\mathcal{L}^{C}(\cdot\mid x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, through a code-data synthesis pipeline. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> works with any such pipeline, and we use a typical open-source one <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Each candidate must then compile or render through a per-format gatekeeper, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}=\operatorname{Exec}(c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Exec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}=\operatorname{Exec}(c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and candidates that still fail after regeneration are set aside in a repair pool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A rubric generator <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns each candidate its difficulty level and its code and visual rubrics (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), and a separate judge <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>J</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{J}</annotation></semantics></math> scores the code against <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(c)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(c)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the render against <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The modality scores and the overall score <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> follow Eqs. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We use distinct models for rubric generation and judging so that biases in rubric construction are not reproduced during scoring.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We then gate candidates by their overall scores. Those with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}&lt;\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}&lt;\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are discarded, those with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}\leq S_{i}&lt;\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}\leq S_{i}&lt;\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are sent back for re-synthesis, and those with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\geq\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\geq\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are retained in the curated pool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The synthesis pipeline serves this gate in two ways. It re-rolls the code of a mid-band candidate with the failed rubric items as feedback, and it synthesizes new samples seeded by retained candidates. Both kinds of output re-enter the pipeline at the execution gate and are scored and routed like any other candidate. For the final supervised corpus, we retain a sample only if its score meets the threshold for its domain, <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\geq\tau_{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\geq\tau_{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">4.3. RL Data Curation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning draws on a compact set of instructions held out from the supervised
pool, which SFT never sees. Among them we keep the medium or hard instructions on which the
SFT policy sometimes succeeds and sometimes fails. Such instructions sit near the policy’s
capability boundary, and their rollouts differ in quality enough to give group-relative
optimization a contrast to learn from, whereas instructions that every rollout passes or
fails provide none.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the SFT-tuned policy. For each candidate instance <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we draw <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> independent responses
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{i}^{(k)}\sim\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(\cdot\mid x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{(k)}\sim\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(\cdot\mid x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluate each rollout using the rubric-based scoring function defined in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
We then measure the informativeness of instance <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> by the empirical dispersion of its rubric
scores</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\left(S_{i}^{(k)}-\bar{S}_{i}\right)^{2},\quad\bar{S}_{i}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}S_{i}^{(k)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\left(S_{i}^{(k)}-\bar{S}_{i}\right)^{2},\quad\bar{S}_{i}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}S_{i}^{(k)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A large <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> means that the policy sometimes succeeds and sometimes fails on the same
instruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we run RL on all Python, HTML, and SVG tasks, and for each domain we hold out
20K <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">medium</em> or <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hard</em> candidates before SFT. We then draw <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=5</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts per instance from <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math>, score
each rollout against its code and visual rubrics, and retain the 5K instances with the
largest <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. RL thus concentrates on the policy’s capability frontier at a fraction
of the rollout cost of a larger candidate pool.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">5. Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">5.1. Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We release <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B-A3B (abbreviated as <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B), post-trained from
Qwen3.5-9B and Qwen3.5-35B-A3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2026</a>)</cite>. We also train
Gemma-4-31B-it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemma Team, 2026</a>)</cite> and Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
More training details are in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Training Details ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Pipeline Details.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We reprocess data from prior open releases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> treats its
models as pluggable, and in this paper we use <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-Opus-4.6</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026a</a>)</cite> as the
curation model and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini-3-Pro</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemini Team, 2025</a>)</cite> as the rubric generator. Both data gating
and reinforcement learning use Gemini 3 Flash as the judge.
The sources are listed per category in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Data Sources ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Besides Qwen3.5, we compare against open multimodal code models, namely JanusCoderV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, VinciCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and VisCoder2-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">5.2. Benchmarking</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Vision-Centric Settings.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The vision-centric suite comprises
ChartMimic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, WebCode2M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
DesignBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Design2Code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and
ChemDraw <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, together with the image-conditioned
lenses of InteractScience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> (VQT) and UniSVG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (ISVGen).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text-Centric Settings.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The text-centric suite comprises
ArtifactsBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, PandasPlotBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galimzyanov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
and VisPlotBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni et al., 2026</a>)</cite> in eight visualization languages, together with
InteractScience’s programmatic lens (PFT) and UniSVG’s text-conditioned lens (TSVGen).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Protocol.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation follows each benchmark’s official protocol, with
deterministic metrics reported alongside judge scores. Since some designated judges share a
model family with our pipeline, we check that the gains hold across judges
(<a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3. Rubric Agreement with Humans ‣ 6. Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>). Details are presented in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Benchmark Details and Evaluation Settings ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">5.3. Vision-Centric Results</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Web Interfaces.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B lead open baseline on front-end generation, achieving judge scores more than 20 points higher than those of task-specific open baselines (<a href="#S5.T2" title="In Web Interfaces. ‣ 5.3. Vision-Centric Results ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B also scores above proprietary model on DesignBench generation and WebCode2M CLIP score. DesignBench editing is the only web-interface metric on which both models score below all Claude 4.6 and Gemini 3 variants.</p>
