<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">6. Analysis</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">6.1. Rubric Efficacy</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To intuitively demonstrate the advantages of the rubric-based method over direct judging, we fine-tune Qwen3.5-9B on two equal-sized subsets drawn from the same data pool.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.20538v1/filter_ablation.svg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/98;" width="476" height="98"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">PrismaForge<span id="S6.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> filtering outperforms direct-judge filtering on every benchmark.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One is filtered by direct scoring, where a judge assigns each
sample a single holistic score as in a typical pipeline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, and the
other by the <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> rubric. The model trained on the rubric-filtered subset scores higher on
every benchmark, by as much as 24 points on Vega-Lite (<a href="#S6.F5" title="In 6.1. Rubric Efficacy ‣ 6. Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). The
gains are largest on the text-centric formats and small on the vision-centric benchmarks,
where Design2Code moves by a fraction of a point. Since both subsets have the same size, the gap
reflects which samples each judge keeps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">6.2. Backbone Generalization</h3>

<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.20538v1/backbone_stages.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/165;" width="476" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Per-stage gains of our SFT and RL pipeline over two base models on every metric.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the released Qwen3.5 backbones, we apply the same data and method to
Gemma-4-31B-it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemma Team, 2026</a>)</cite> and Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>)</cite> on
Python plotting and HTML, the two most represented domains. On both backbones all twelve metrics
end above the base model, and RL adds to SFT on every one (<a href="#S6.F6" title="In 6.2. Backbone Generalization ‣ 6. Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). SFT
carries most of the ChartMimic gain, whereas RL drives most of the HTML gains and recovers every
metric that dips after SFT alone. More results on Gemma are presented in Appendix <a href="#A8.SS1" title="H.1. Backbone Generalization ‣ Appendix H Extended Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">6.3. Rubric Agreement with Humans</h3>

<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:47%;"><img src="2610.20538v1/judge_agreement.png" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/114;" width="229" height="114" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S6.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">PrismaForge<span id="S6.F7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> scoring aligns judges with humans better than direct
scoring.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because multimodal code artifacts are ultimately created for humans, consistency with human judgment provides a compelling test to verify that the quality of data yielded by our rubric-based PrismaForge aligns more closely with human preferences.
At the same time, preserving the relative ordering induced by scores is particularly important for reward-based policy optimization, where inconsistent rankings can provide conflicting training signals.
To evaluate our method along both dimensions, we conducted a human study. We sampled 100 examples from our training data and asked human annotators, as well as three proprietary model judges, to evaluate them using direct scoring and the <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> rubrics, respectively. We then measured the agreement between each model judge and the human ratings in terms of both absolute score levels and pairwise ordering (<a href="#S6.F7" title="In 6.3. Rubric Agreement with Humans ‣ 6. Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). The rubric-based judging method achieves substantially higher agreement with human judgments and greater consistency in the induced partial ordering across all three model judges. The full study protocol and detailed statistics are provided in Appendix <a href="#A8.SS2" title="H.2. Cross-Family Adjudication and Human Evaluation ‣ Appendix H Extended Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.2</span></a>.
We further show that the gains of our pipeline hold across judge models, with the
experiments presented in Appendix <a href="#A8.SS1" title="H.1. Backbone Generalization ‣ Appendix H Extended Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">7. Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we push open multimodal code intelligence forward by jointly
contributing and fully releasing data, pipeline, and models. <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> treats
multimodal code data under one lens with rubrics synthesized from coarse to fine
and decomposed into code-level and visual-level criteria, a signal that both
generalizes and localizes what went wrong.
We then curate and release <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M, the most broadly covering open dataset
for multimodal code intelligence, which enables principled selection and stable reinforcement learning on
visual-coding tasks. On this data we build <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B/35B, which lead the open state of the art
by a wide margin and rival proprietary systems. Extensive analyses across base models and task recipes show that the data and
the pipeline generalize beyond our release, and we share these insights and resources
to broaden access to visual-coding capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
