<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Data Toolkit Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">A.1. AST-Based Decomposition</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Plotting scripts collected from GitHub and Manim scene files are often long, and one file may
draw several figures.
For such data, we parse each script into an abstract syntax tree without executing it and split it into self-contained units. A unit is the code that produces one figure in a plotting script, or one scene in a Manim file (a class inheriting from <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Scene</span>). Each unit keeps the imports and statements it depends on,
drops project-private helpers that exist only in the source repository, and carries its embedded
text and a short excerpt as features. The units are stored as structured entries that the
synthesis pipeline and the gatekeeper of Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Iterative Data Gating ‣ 4. PrismaCoder-2M ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> read. All of these processing pipelines will be made public.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">A.2. Per-Format Gatekeepers</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every candidate runs in a sandbox matched to its language before it is judged. Python plotting
code executes headlessly and must save a figure, and HTML pages render in a headless browser to a
screenshot. SVG, <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span>, Mermaid, Asymptote, LilyPond, and Vega-Lite sources go through their
standard renderers or compilers, Manim scenes render to their frames, and chemical-structure code
must parse into a valid molecule. A candidate passes when the run finishes within a fixed time
limit and produces its artifact, and it fails on an error, a timeout, or an empty artifact. The
same gatekeeper serves data gating (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Iterative Data Gating ‣ 4. PrismaCoder-2M ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) and reinforcement learning
(<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Rubric-Based Policy Optimization ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), where a failed rollout receives reward <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">A.3. Decontamination</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Part of <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M is synthesized by frontier models that may have seen public benchmarks, so we
decontaminate the released data against the test sets of every benchmark in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Benchmarking ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
A training sample is removed when its instruction shares a long <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-gram with a test instruction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
