<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Holistic judgment.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The holistic evaluator assigns a score of <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>78</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">78</annotation></semantics></math> with this rationale:</p>
<blockquote id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The rendered image matches the reference closely in fonts, colors, and general
structure. However, there is a layout error where the footer is positioned too
high, overlapping the “Service 3” button and leaving empty space at the
bottom.</span></p>
</blockquote>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, the holistic evaluator notices the dominant layout error but compresses
it into a relatively high scalar score. The rubric-based evaluation both
amplifies its impact according to sample-specific requirements and identifies
the additional corner-shape and content-visibility failures. These localized
but perceptually important errors are easily diluted in a single holistic
similarity score, whereas our fine-grained rubrics explicitly expose and
penalize each failure. The two rubric
views thus provide more diagnostic and structurally sensitive feedback than
code inspection or holistic visual judgment alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we use Gemini-3-Flash for rubrics. For common visualization tasks, such as Python plotting, the average cost of generating rubrics for a single sample is approximately $0.006 based on the official API pricing, while evaluating the generated code and visual artifacts costs approximately $0.01 per sample. For HTML-related tasks, the input cost is substantially higher because rich, complex websites typically involve much longer HTML code. In these cases, rubric generation costs approximately $0.01 per sample, while evaluation costs approximately $0.04.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>RL Reward Specification</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix specifies the reward computation procedure behind <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Rubric-Based Policy Optimization ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each rollout response <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the procedure first extracts code from the response text. For Python, we extract the longest fenced code block. For HTML, we extract a single complete document spanning from the doctype declaration to the closing tag. For SVG, we extract either the code contained in a fenced block or the first complete <span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;svg&gt;</span> span. A completion that does not contain valid code of the required type is assigned a reward of <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> without further evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A dispatcher then routes each rollout to exactly one task family, and the scorer registered for that family returns a reward in <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a fully satisfactory solution. An unscoreable completion (due to a missing or malformed answer, unavailable rubric or reference material, execution or rendering failure, or a judge failure that persists after retries) receives a reward of <math id="A4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The basic reward computation for each task largely follows Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Specifically, the response code and the rendered visual artifact are scored separately and then aggregated using a weighted average. We use different combinations of code-score and visual-score weights across task families. This is because for tasks such as Python plotting, correct data representation and placement are as important as visual integrity and presentation quality. In contrast, for educational demonstration artifacts, visual readiness and interactivity are often more important than the particular implementation used to produce them.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We found, however, that predefined rubrics alone may fail to capture certain forms of misalignment in a few tasks such as interface reproduction. Instructions for these tasks inherently allow some flexibility in content generation, particularly with respect to styling and layout organization. As a result, some errors or mismatches can only be assessed reliably after the generated page is viewed alongside the reference page. To address this limitation, we introduce a complementary holistic score for task families of this type. This score is combined with the rubric-based scores using a weight denoted by <math id="A4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way, the evaluation retains the consistency and structure of rubric-based scoring while also capturing qualitative discrepancies that become apparent only when the generated artifact is evaluated as a whole.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The reward logic and the weight combinations used for each task domain are summarized in <a href="#A4.T7" title="In Appendix D RL Reward Specification ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For each sample, the rubric-based method requires one API call to score each modality. Because code and visual artifacts are evaluated separately, its API cost is approximately <math id="A4.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> that of directly scoring the visual outputs alone. In our experiments, a full round of GRPO training involves approximately 808K judgment requests. Resource constraints prevented a full GRPO comparison between <span id="A4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span> and direct scoring. In a focused Python plotting experiment, rubric-based scoring improved ChartMimic overall by 6.91 points over direct scoring.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A4.T7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="A4.T7.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">RL rewards by task family. Rewards lie in <math id="A4.T7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>;
