<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Benchmark Details and Evaluation Settings</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Code-driven video and animation, and theorem and symbolic visualization, are released as
training data only and are not part of the evaluation.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Vision-Centric Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ChartMimic reproduces a chart from its
image; its Direct split regenerates the chart and its Customized split edits an
existing one, scored by low-level element extraction (text, layout, type, color)
and a high-level judge score. WebCode2M, DesignBench, and Design2Code cover
front-end generation from a screenshot: WebCode2M buckets pages by length and
reports a judge score, visual similarity, CLIP, and TreeBLEU; DesignBench scores
generation and editing with a judge score plus CLIP, MAE, SSIM, and CMS;
Design2Code reports element-level block, text, position, and color matches, CLIP,
and a judge score. InteractScience’s visual lens (VQT) is a visually grounded
qualitative test judged by a vision–language model. UniSVG’s image-conditioned
lens (ISVGen) reproduces an image as SVG. ChemDraw measures execution rate,
molecule validity, exact match, and Tanimoto similarity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text-Centric Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ArtifactsBench reports an overall score
over ten artifact domains. PandasPlotBench reports the incorrect-code rate and
mean and Good(<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>75) scores under a visual and a task lens. InteractScience’s
programmatic lens (PFT) is a functional test over the generated demonstration.
UniSVG’s text-conditioned lens (TSVGen) generates SVG from text. VisPlotBench
covers eight languages with the same metrics as PandasPlotBench, using its designated
judge GPT-4.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Settings.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation follows the benchmark’s official
protocol, including its designated judge model where one is specified, so that
our numbers stay comparable with published results.
Due to the availability of legacy models, ChartMimic’s high-level score is
judged by GPT-5.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026</a>)</cite> instead of the original <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Training Details</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments were conducted on four interconnected compute nodes, each equipped with 8 NVIDIA H200 GPUs, for a total of 32 GPUs. We used the Megatron backend in the ms-swift framework for distributed SFT and RL. Both stages performed full-parameter fine-tuning with FlashAttention and sequence parallelism enabled. Activation recomputation reduced memory use throughout training.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For SFT, we initialized from Qwen3.5-9B/Qwen3.5-35B-A3B and trained the language model for 5 epochs. We used a maximum sequence length of 32,768 tokens and enabled sequence packing for improved token utilization. The global batch size was set to be 128. The learning rate peaked at <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a warm-up fraction of 5%, and decayed to a minimum learning rate of <math id="A6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For RL, we initialized from the SFT checkpoint and performed one epoch of GRPO/GSPO. We implemented a rubric-based reward plugin following the methodology described in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Rubric-Based Policy Optimization ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D RL Reward Specification ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. For the judge model <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>J</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{J}</annotation></semantics></math> used to evaluate online rollouts, we selected Gemini-3-Flash to balance evaluation quality and cost. The global training batch size was 512 in terms of rollouts (equivalent to 64 samples per batch) and 8 candidate responses were generated per prompt at each rollout step. Rollout generation was performed with colocated vLLM inference using tensor parallelism of size 8 and a maximum model length of 36,000 tokens. Sampling used a temperature of 1.0 and top-<math id="A6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.95.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The GRPO/GSPO learning rate was set to <math id="A6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. We used a KL regularization coefficient of <math id="A6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.04</annotation></semantics></math>, and asymmetric clipping thresholds of <math id="A6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0.20</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{high}}=0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{high}}=0.28</annotation></semantics></math>. Dynamic sampling and overlong-response filtering were enabled during rollout collection. Training was conducted in BF16 precision, with model and optimizer offloading enabled to reduce GPU memory usage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Full Per-Benchmark Results</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each benchmark reports several metrics; the main text shows a compact headline set
per benchmark, and the complete metric sets are here, one table per benchmark,
mirroring our evaluation workbook.</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">G.1. Visualization</h3>

<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ChartMimic.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The complete results of the direct-1800 split of the ChartMimic benchmark is shown in Table <a href="#A7.T8" title="Tab. 8 ‣ ChartMimic. ‣ G.1. Visualization ‣ Appendix G Full Per-Benchmark Results ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. We can see that PrismaCoder shows strong performance against both open and proprietary models. PrismaCoder-9B achieves an overall score of 77.70, substantially outperforming other similarly sized open models (around 72–73) and even surpassing GPT-4o (74.20). Scaling to PrismaCoder-35B further improves the overall score to 82.05, with particularly strong execution rate (96.16), text fidelity (90.41), layout (94.00), and GPT-based similarity (80.69). Its overall performance is competitive with much larger proprietary models, exceeding Claude Sonnet 4.6 and Claude Opus 4.6 in this evaluation, while approaching Gemini 3 Flash. These results suggest that PrismaCoder is especially effective at faithfully reconstructing chart structure and visual details while maintaining reliable executable code.</p>
</div>
