<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">PandasPlotBench.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A7.T9.fig1" title="Tab. 9 ‣ PandasPlotBench. ‣ G.1. Visualization ‣ Appendix G Full Per-Benchmark Results ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> illustrates a more detailed results of PandasPlotBench. On this benchmark, PrismaCoder demonstrates competitive visualization-code generation performance across both model scales. PrismaCoder-9B achieves a mean visual/task score of 60.54/77.94 with 14.29% incorrect code, comparing favorably with other models in a similar size range, while PrismaCoder-35B further reduces the incorrect-code rate to 9.29% and reaches 63.38/79.85, with 60.6% of examples attaining a visual score above 75. These gains are relatively modest compared with some strong baselines, which is unsurprising given the nature of the benchmark. Introduced in 2024, the benchmark contains 175 human-curated, largely Matplotlib-gallery-derived tasks and evaluates plotting from controlled natural-language descriptions, including synthetically shortened variants. In contrast, much of PrismaCoder’s training data is curated toward more realistic and diverse visualization workflows. We include this classic Python visualization benchmark to assess our models on established plotting tasks.</p>
</div>
