<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">G.2. WebDev</h3>

<section id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">WebCode2M.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A7.T14" title="Tab. 14 ‣ WebCode2M. ‣ G.2. WebDev ‣ Appendix G Full Per-Benchmark Results ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> shows that <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>’s advantage is consistent across webpages of different code lengths.
<span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B obtains Visual scores of 90.06, 90.02, and 91.15 on the short, mid, and long subsets, respectively, while its LLM score remains similarly stable across the three groups.
CLIP similarity decreases modestly with page length, from 90.29 to 87.32, but remains well above the prior open models throughout.
The main exception is TreeBLEU: both <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span> models score below JanusCoder and VinciCoder despite producing substantially more faithful renderings according to the visual metrics.
This difference arises because TreeBLEU measures similarity to the reference HTML tree rather than the rendered webpage itself.
JanusCoder and VinciCoder are trained on the original WebCode2M code, which naturally favors reproducing structures similar to the benchmark references.
<span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span> instead uses regenerated code paired with webpages rendered from that code, rather than directly using the original WebCode2M source code.
This regeneration is intentional.
The original WebCode2M code comes from diverse real-world webpages and can differ substantially from the code distribution naturally produced by LLMs.
Using regenerated code therefore better aligns the training targets with inference-time generations, while re-rendering the regenerated programs ensures exact consistency between the code and its visual target.
Its generated webpages can consequently closely match the target appearance without following the particular HTML or DOM structure of the reference implementation.
The similarly low TreeBLEU scores of the proprietary models further illustrate that reference-code similarity does not necessarily track rendering fidelity.</p>
</div>
