<section id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Design2Code.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A7.T16.fig1" title="Tab. 16 ‣ Design2Code. ‣ G.2. WebDev ‣ Appendix G Full Per-Benchmark Results ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> provides a finer-grained view of the visual gains on Design2Code.
<span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B reaches 90.09 in Block, 95.95 in Text, 89.30 in Position, 90.00 in Color, and 92.37 in CLIP, yielding an average Visual score of 91.54.
Relative to VinciCoder-8B, the largest gains appear in Position, Color, and Block, which improve by 15.40, 13.00, and 10.68 points, respectively, whereas the improvements in Text and CLIP are smaller.
Under the Design2Code evaluation, these dimensions respectively capture spatial alignment of matched elements, perceptual color fidelity, and the coverage of visual blocks while penalizing missing or hallucinated elements.
The results therefore indicate that <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>’s advantage comes primarily from recovering a more complete set of webpage elements and placing them more accurately, together with improved color matching, rather than simply reproducing the visible text.
<span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B exhibits a very similar metric profile, with only minor differences between the two model sizes across the evaluated dimensions.</p>
