<section id="A7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">G.3. Artifacts and Demos</h3>

<section id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ArtifactsBench.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A7.T17" title="Tab. 17 ‣ ArtifactsBench. ‣ G.3. Artifacts and Demos ‣ Appendix G Full Per-Benchmark Results ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> presents the overall and domain-level performance on ArtifactsBench. The results show that our models generalize well across the heterogeneous artifact types in ArtifactsBench. Rather than being concentrated in a single domain, <span id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B maintains strong performance across all five categories and achieves the highest overall score among the open models, with particularly strong results on SVG generation and web applications. <span id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B reaches a similar overall level but shows a different strength profile, with especially strong performance on game development and simulation. This contrast suggests that the two models are capable of handling different forms of front-end complexity, from visual and interface construction to artifacts that require richer interaction and application logic. More importantly, both models consistently outperform the other open baselines and the proprietary baseline at the overall level, despite ArtifactsBench spanning substantially different artifact structures. This cross-domain consistency indicates that their advantage is not tied to a specific artifact format, but reflects stronger general front-end code generation and the ability to turn complex specifications into executable artifacts.</p>
</div>
