<section id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">InteractScience.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A7.T18.fig1" title="Tab. 18 ‣ InteractScience. ‣ G.3. Artifacts and Demos ‣ Appendix G Full Per-Benchmark Results ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> shows full metrics on InteractScience for both PFT and VQT lenses. The results highlight the complementary strengths of our models captured by the two lenses of InteractScience. PFT measures whether a model can correctly implement the interactive components specified in a long implementation plan, while VQT places greater emphasis on integrating visual understanding, executable code, and scientific reasoning. Our models perform particularly strongly under this setting. <span id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-9B and <span id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-35B both achieve substantially higher VQT scores than the other open models, while also maintaining competitive PFT performance. This gap is important because success on VQT cannot be obtained simply by producing syntactically valid front-end code. The generated science demos must also reflect the intended visual state and the scientific relationships encoded in the task. The results therefore point to a useful property of our models whose capability is not limited to implementing isolated UI interactions, but extends to coordinating code, visual requirements, and scientific content when following a detailed implementation plan.</p>
</div>
