<section id="A8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">H.1. Backbone Generalization</h3>

<div id="A8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A8.F10" title="In H.1. Backbone Generalization ‣ Appendix H Extended Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> restates the Gemma arm of <a href="#S6.F6" title="In 6.2. Backbone Generalization ‣ 6. Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> as
dumbbells, with the base and final score of every metric. The dumbbells express every
metric on a percentage scale, so the change in the 0–10 DesignBench Edit score is
plotted <math id="A8.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> while its score columns keep the native scale.
<a href="#A8.T19" title="In H.1. Backbone Generalization ‣ Appendix H Extended Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> scores
the VisPlotBench splits under two judges, the benchmark’s designated
GPT-4.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> and the newer GPT-5.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026</a>)</cite>. The two
agree on the direction of every training effect. The one disagreement is the HTML task
score, where GPT-4.1 ranks the RL checkpoint first and GPT-5.5 the SFT one.</p>
</div>
