<section id="A8.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B11B34;">H.2. Cross-Family Adjudication and Human Evaluation</h3>

<div id="A8.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A8.T20" title="In H.2. Cross-Family Adjudication and Human Evaluation ‣ Appendix H Extended Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> lists the agreement statistics of the human study of
<a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3. Rubric Agreement with Humans ‣ 6. Analysis ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>. The 100 samples come from the SFT split of <span id="A8.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaCoder</span>-2M,
sampled by difficulty level with Python and HTML artifacts in equal numbers across
tasks. Twenty annotators each rated ten samples directly and ten others through the
rubric, with disjoint sample sets per annotator, so each sample carries two human
ratings under each mode. Each of the three judges, Claude Sonnet
5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2026b</a>)</cite>, Gemini 3.5 Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemini Team, 2026</a>)</cite>, and
GPT-5.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026</a>)</cite>, scored every sample twice under each mode. The
direct mode asks for a single holistic score, as in a typical
pipeline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>; the rubric mode scores through the <span id="A8.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PrismaForge</span>
rubric of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Elastic Rubric Scorer ‣ 3. PrismaForge ‣ PrismaCoder: Pushing the Boundaries of Open Multimodal Code Intelligence through Decomposed Rubric Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Krippendorff’s <math id="A8.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and Kendall’s <math id="A8.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> are
computed between each judge’s scores and the human ratings under the same mode. Across
the three judges, <math id="A8.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> rises from 0.30–0.51 under direct scoring to 0.86–0.92
under the rubric, and <math id="A8.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> from 0.28–0.44 to 0.64–0.67.</p>
</div>
